---
description: Stability and memory rules for ESP32/C++ (embedded)
globs: *.cpp, *.h, *.ino, *.c
alwaysApply: true
---

# Target System
- ESP32-C3 (RISC-V, single-core, 400 KB RAM, 4 MB flash), Arduino framework, HomeSpan
- Device runs 24/7 unattended – stability takes priority over elegance and feature scope

# Dynamic Memory / Heap Fragmentation
- NO Arduino `String` class in loops or recurring functions –
  use fixed-size `char` arrays with `snprintf()` instead
- No repeated `malloc`/`free` or `new`/`delete` at runtime –
  allocate buffers once at startup (statically or in setup())
- No `std::string`, `std::vector` etc. with growing size in the loop;
  if containers are needed: `reserve()` at startup, never let size grow dynamically
- For JSON: ArduinoJson with statically sized document
  (`StaticJsonDocument` / fixed capacity), justify the chosen size

# Buffer Overflows
- Never use `strcpy`, `strcat`, `sprintf`, `gets` – always `strlcpy`, `strlcat`,
  `snprintf` with sizeof(buffer) as the limit
- Always check array index access against buffer size
- When receiving via Serial/WiFi/MQTT: validate length BEFORE copying,
  always null-terminate input

# Stack
- No large local arrays (> 256 bytes) inside functions – declare them `static`
  or global instead
- No recursion
- For custom FreeRTOS tasks: size stacks generously (min. 4096) and verify
  with `uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark()`

# Blocking & Watchdog
- No `delay()` > 50 ms in the main loop – use millis()-based
  state machines (non-blocking) instead
- No endless loops without `yield()` or `vTaskDelay()`
- ALWAYS add a timeout to `while` loops waiting on hardware/network
- WiFi/network operations must never block the HomeSpan loop

# Robustness / Error Handling
- Always check return values of network, file, and init functions –
  no silent ignoring
- After connection loss (WiFi/MQTT): automatic reconnect with backoff,
  never reboot as the first resort
- Handle millis() rollover (after ~49 days) correctly:
  `if (millis() - lastTime >= interval)` – never absolute time comparisons
- Provide `ESP.getFreeHeap()` and `ESP.getMinFreeHeap()` as diagnostics
  (e.g. periodic log) so leaks are caught early

# Concurrency / ISRs
- Keep interrupt handlers minimal: only set a flag, mark with IRAM_ATTR,
  no Serial output, no malloc, no delay inside ISRs
- Declare variables shared between ISR and loop as `volatile`
- With FreeRTOS: share data via queues/mutexes, no unprotected globals

# General
- Take compiler warnings seriously – code must compile warning-free
- Constants instead of magic numbers (prefer `constexpr`)
- For every change: briefly justify why it is memory- and runtime-safe
